  "contentHtml": "<figure><a href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!O9X7!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2a05103c-1d91-447e-a9f4-648dedd4c605_1900x1267.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!O9X7!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F2a05103c-1d91-447e-a9f4-648dedd4c605_1900x1267.png\" width=\"1456\" height=\"971\" data-size=\"normal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><figcaption><strong>Kris Bryant #17 and Anthony Rizzo #44 of the Chicago Cubs celebrate after defeating the Cleveland Indians 8-7 in Game Seven of the 2016 World Series at Progressive Field on November 2, 2016 in Cleveland, Ohio. The Cubs win their first World Series in 108 years.</strong> (Ezra Shaw/Getty Images)</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Back in November of 2016, plenty of us were trying to imagine what the next decade would bring. Ten years later, it’s fair to say the timeline we got wasn’t exactly the one many of us expected… By which I mean, we thought the Chicago Cubs <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-cubs-ended-their-108-year-drought?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">would use their 2016 World Series title</a> as a <a href=\"https://www.theringer.com/2016/11/29/mlb/how-the-cubs-can-become-a-dynasty-b8a61245d849#.l5y4kgb7n\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">springboard to a dynasty</a>. (What else did you <em>think </em>I meant?)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/subscribe?\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p>\n<p>I mean, we already had a story written for what would surely happen next. <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/theo-epsteins-curse-breakers?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Theo Epstein had </a><em><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/theo-epsteins-curse-breakers?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">just </a></em><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/theo-epsteins-curse-breakers?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">helped Boston end its championship drought</a> — then win <a href=\"https://www.baseball-reference.com/teams/BOS/2007.shtml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">again</a>. (And <a href=\"https://www.baseball-reference.com/teams/BOS/2013.shtml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">again</a>, kinda.)<a href=\"#footnote-1\" class=\"reader-footnote-ref\" aria-label=\"Footnote 1\">1</a> Now Epstein had applied his brand of magic to Chicago, <a href=\"https://www.espn.com/espn/feature/story/_/id/17711479/how-theo-epstein-transformed-cubs-roster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">remaking the team’s entire roster</a> in the span of a few years after being hired by the Cubs in October 2011. Having watched him turn one curse-breaking team into a repeat champion, it was easy to assume he could just do it again.</p>\n<p>Here’s how the Cubs’ year <a href=\"https://www.baseball-reference.com/teams/CHC/pos.shtml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">lineups</a> and <a href=\"https://www.baseball-reference.com/teams/CHC/staff.shtml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pitching staffs</a> changed from Epstein’s first season through the 2016 championship, with each regular’s <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/2026-mlb-elo-power-ratings-playoff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">JEFFBAGWELL WAR per 162 team games</a> shown and players who appeared for the title team marked with a cute lil trophy emoji 🏆:</p>\n<p>\n          <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-2016-cubs-and-the-future-we-took\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n              Read more\n          </a>\n      </p>",
